<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#caravan-trade">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caravan trade</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as caravan routes, caravan commerce, caravan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caravan trade is long-distance overland commerce carried by groups of merchants with pack animals, typically <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, travelling together for safety. It linked the trans-Saharan routes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a> empires and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> networks of the Timurid and Seljuk empires. Control and taxation of caravan routes was a major source of wealth for many states, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a>.</p></div>
